1
00:00:00,257 --> 00:00:05,249
In 1928, a scientist came back from
holiday to a pile of dirty dishes.

2
00:00:05,259 --> 00:00:07,759
One of them was about to change the world.

3
00:00:07,866 --> 00:00:09,875
A fuzzy mould had landed on it.

4
00:00:09,885 --> 00:00:12,710
And all around the mould,
the bacteria were dead.

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,926
Here's why.

6
00:00:13,936 --> 00:00:17,754
A bacterium lives inside a wall
it keeps rebuilding as it grows.

7
00:00:17,764 --> 00:00:20,157
The mould's chemical jams the builders.

8
00:00:20,167 --> 00:00:22,742
The wall cracks, and the bacterium bursts.

9
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:24,805
Fleming called it penicillin.

10
00:00:24,815 --> 00:00:28,155
But he could barely make any,
and for ten years, almost nobody

11
00:00:28,165 --> 00:00:29,440
paid attention.

12
00:00:29,588 --> 00:00:32,673
Then, in Oxford, a team
finally purified it.

13
00:00:32,683 --> 00:00:35,464
Their first real test
was a dying policeman.

14
00:00:35,475 --> 00:00:37,712
Within days, he was getting better.

15
00:00:37,723 --> 00:00:39,598
Then the penicillin ran out.

16
00:00:39,683 --> 00:00:42,448
They were so desperate, they
pulled it back out of his urine

17
00:00:42,458 --> 00:00:43,710
to use it again.

18
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:44,635
It wasn't enough.

19
00:00:44,645 --> 00:00:45,645
He died.

20
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:49,272
So the hunt moved to America,
where the best penicillin mould

21
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:52,472
they ever found turned up on a
rotten cantaloupe from a market

22
00:00:52,482 --> 00:00:53,582
in Illinois.

23
00:00:53,668 --> 00:00:56,192
By D-Day, there were millions of doses.

24
00:00:56,369 --> 00:01:00,661
Fleming warned that if we misused it,
bacteria would learn to resist it.

25
00:01:00,671 --> 00:01:01,821
He was right.

26
00:01:01,924 --> 00:01:05,799
The next miracle might already be
growing in someone's dirty dishes.
